"""Original non-clinical first-response teaching for fictional adult disclosures."""
def course(c,m):
 return c("SWP-050","Survivor Centred First Response and Referral","survivor-centred-first-response-and-referral","Survivor Centred\nFirst Response\nand Referral",
 "Demonstrate a bounded first response to a fictional adult disclosure, preserving choice, privacy and role limits while offering verified referral information and appropriate urgent support.",[
m("A safe first response to disclosure","Offer respectful basic support without investigating, forcing a story or creating an incident record.",[
"A first response begins when a person voluntarily asks for help or shares a concern. It is not an invitation to search for survivors, conduct an interview for evidence or require a detailed account. Introduce the role honestly, ask what support the person wants now and consider whether the setting allows a private conversation without increasing risk. The GBV Pocket Guide addresses non-specialists when a GBV actor is unavailable and emphasises basic support rather than investigation. This course uses fictional adult situations; it does not authorise learners to practise by seeking real disclosures in their community. [S12, S1]",
"Listen without blame and without requiring the person to repeat distressing details. A supportive response recognises the concern and the person's right to respectful assistance. Avoid questions that suggest responsibility for another person's violence, promises that everything will be resolved or pressure to accept the worker's preferred option. Ask only what is necessary to understand the immediate request and appropriate next support. The specialist case-management guidance provides a fuller professional framework; an introductory worker should not turn a brief disclosure into a specialist intake simply because a form is available. [S11, S12]",
"Privacy includes the environment and information handling. A person may not want a companion, an interpreter drawn from their household or a phone recording the discussion. Do not photograph, transcribe or keep identifying incident details as a learning exercise. Understand applicable confidentiality limits and explain the relevant limits honestly before seeking sensitive information; do not invent a universal rule or promise secrecy without exceptions. Consultation should use an authorised, necessary process with consent and non-identifying information wherever possible. If a real situation raises safeguarding or urgent concerns, follow the relevant qualified pathway rather than improvise from a classroom scenario. [S12, S11, S1]",
"WHO's health-worker training overview highlights compassionate communication and first-line support, with further skills practice. It is a comparator for professional learning, not evidence that this non-clinical course qualifies a health provider. The provider-perspective synthesis supports attention to safety, agency and relationships, but does not establish that a particular phrase guarantees recovery. In the fictional task, practise a brief introduction, a non-blaming acknowledgement, a question about the immediate need and an explanation of the role. A learner may pause or use an alternative fictional task instead of performing a distressing role-play. [S13, R22, S1]"],
"Fictional case: an adult at a service desk quietly asks for information after describing controlling behaviour. The worker offers an appropriate private conversation, explains the role and relevant limits, listens to the immediate request and avoids requesting a detailed incident history or making a recording.",
"Write a short fictional response with a role introduction, a non-blaming acknowledgement, an immediate-needs question and a statement of limits. Identify one detail the non-specialist should not collect and one environmental privacy check.",
"The response should make space for the person's request and choice without investigating, recording identifying incident details or promising outcomes. It should keep health treatment, specialist intake and safeguarding decisions with the appropriate roles.",["S12","S1","S11","S13","R22"],[
("Non-specialist support follows voluntary disclosure.","A learner is told to find survivors to practise interviewing. What fits the course's first-response boundary?","Use the supplied fictional tasks and be prepared to respond appropriately if someone independently requests help.",["Seek out survivors and collect detailed histories for marks.","Require applicants to disclose violence before any assistance.","Treat a classroom exercise as permission to investigate."],"The course does not authorise proactive survivor identification or investigation."),
("Support does not require a detailed retelling.","An adult asks for service information after a brief disclosure. What should guide further questions?","Only what is necessary for the immediate request and appropriate support within role.",["A requirement to recount every incident in detail.","The worker's curiosity about the relationship.","A need to complete an evidence statement before listening."],"A non-specialist response should not force unnecessary recounting."),
("A first response should avoid blame.","Which response best supports an adult who describes coercion?","You deserve respectful support; what would be helpful to you now?",["You should have prevented the other person's behaviour.","Help is available only if you make the decision I prefer.","Your account matters only if you can prove every detail now."],"The response recognises dignity and invites the person's priority without blame."),
("Non-specialist learning does not require incident records.","A learner wants to record a real disclosure on a phone for coursework. What is appropriate?","Do not record it; use only the approved fictional learning material.",["Record secretly and remove the name later.","Share the recording with classmates for feedback.","Assume an educational purpose removes privacy responsibilities."],"Sensitive personal disclosure is not a course assessment resource."),
("Training comparison is not clinical qualification.","The course links a WHO health-worker curriculum overview. What does that confer on a non-clinical learner?","A curriculum comparison, without authority to provide clinical care.",["A health-provider qualification.","Permission to prescribe treatment.","Proof that an unobserved skill has been mastered."],"A link and short-course completion do not establish clinical competence."),
]),
m("Choice consent and the next support","Offer understandable options and verified information without coercive referral or unsupported guarantees.",[
"Explain available options in language the person can understand, including what remains uncertain. Ask whether the person wants information, help making contact or no referral at this time. Consent to talk is not automatically consent to share identifying details, contact another service or involve a family member. The Pocket Guide and specialist guidelines support respectful choice and informed permission. Relevant legal or safeguarding limits need a locally verified process and clear explanation; this course does not decide reporting law or treat organisational preference as a lawful exception to consent. [S12, S11, S1]",
"Check the receiving pathway before describing it as available. Confirm the current role, opening or response arrangements, accessibility, cost information where known and the safest way to make contact. A directory is a starting point rather than a guarantee of admission or competence. Share what is known and acknowledge what is not verified. Do not promise a place, protection outcome, money or a legal result on another service's behalf. The case-management guidance provides a basis for coordinated referral, while a first responder remains responsible for an accurate account of the actual options and their own limits. [S11, S12]",
"An immediate health or safety concern calls for prompt attention through the appropriate qualified service and applicable urgent-response arrangements, with the person's involvement and consent wherever possible. Do not delay access while completing coursework, obtaining a detailed story or trying to determine a criminal case. Some care can be time-sensitive, so offer accurate information about prompt professional assessment without reproducing medical eligibility cut-offs or treatment instructions. Explain that a clinician or relevant qualified role determines the appropriate assessment and care. A learner should not conclude that help is unavailable because time has passed or an account is incomplete. [S12, S13, S11]",
"If the person declines a proposed contact, maintain respectful support and explain any relevant service availability or lawful limits accurately. Do not make an unauthorised referral secretly or contact an alleged perpetrator to mediate. For children, adolescents, decision-making support or a safeguarding concern, use the appropriate specialist pathway rather than apply an adult-consent exercise mechanically. Provider-perspective research helps identify how coercion can be repeated by services; it does not justify overriding a person's choice in the name of recovery. In the fictional plan, state the chosen next step, verified information and unresolved dependency without retaining real incident details. [R22, S11, S12]"],
"Fictional case: an adult wants information about an accessible service but does not agree to a call today. Its directory entry is old. The worker verifies current information without disclosing the adult's identity, explains uncertainty and respects the decision while identifying how prompt qualified care could be reached if requested.",
"Write a referral-options note for the fictional case. Separate consent to talk, consent to share and consent to make contact. Include a capacity check, an unknown, an urgent professional-support boundary and a response to declining referral.",
"The note should preserve specific permission and accurate information. It must not promise admission, investigate the account, apply medical cut-offs or treat a refusal as permission for secret contact.",["S12","S11","S1","S13","R22"],[
("Permission for one action does not authorise every action.","An adult agrees to a conversation but not information sharing. What can the worker infer?","Permission for the conversation only, within the explained limits.",["Permission to send identifying details to any service.","Automatic consent to contact family.","Consent to publish the account for awareness."],"Consent needs to match the specific proposed action."),
("A referral directory requires current verification.","A service listing is several years old. What should the first responder say?","Its current role and access arrangements need checking before availability is promised.",["Admission is guaranteed because the name appears.","All listed services are equally accessible.","The person must accept it without further information."],"An old listing does not verify present capacity."),
("Prompt qualified care should not wait for a detailed story.","A disclosure raises a potentially urgent health concern. What is within a non-clinical first responder's role?","Facilitate prompt access to qualified assessment through the appropriate arrangements without delaying for a full history.",["Prescribe medication from the course notes.","Delay support until an evidence interview is complete.","Declare care pointless because the account lacks dates."],"Care decisions belong to qualified professionals; unnecessary questioning should not delay access."),
("Declining referral does not authorise secret contact.","An adult declines a proposed service call with no established lawful exception. What respects the decision?","Provide accurate information and respectful support without making the call secretly.",["Call and disclose identifying details anyway.","Contact the alleged perpetrator to negotiate.","Withdraw dignity and non-discriminatory assistance."],"A refusal is not consent, and a worker should not manufacture an exception."),
("Adult exercises do not replace safeguarding expertise.","A fictional scenario is changed to involve a child. What should the learner recognise?","An age-appropriate specialist safeguarding pathway and current local procedures are needed.",["Every adult-consent rule applies unchanged.","Any accompanying adult automatically has safe authority.","The short course resolves all reporting obligations."],"Child and safeguarding situations require appropriate expertise and verified procedures."),
]),
m("Safe handover and bounded follow-up","Describe a permitted handover and feedback route without creating unsafe contact or parallel casework.",[
"A handover should occur only through the agreed appropriate pathway and within the relevant permission and lawful limits. Clarify what the receiving role needs to know for the chosen purpose and avoid transmitting an entire narrative by default. A non-specialist should not create a parallel incident database, retain identifiers for a classroom exercise or circulate a personal account to prove that a referral was made. Specialist guidance requires structured information management in competent services; it does not justify informal records in a learner's phone, email or personal cloud account. [S11, S12, S1]",
"Agree whether any follow-up contact is wanted and what communication would be safe. A shared device, message preview, voicemail or unexpected call can expose a service relationship. Do not assume the contact details given for one administrative purpose are safe for a different purpose. Check the person's preferences and the service's approved process, including what should happen if the contact cannot proceed privately. This lesson does not train a hotline worker or prescribe a universal remote safety protocol. If a safe arrangement cannot be confirmed, seek appropriate specialist advice without improvising a revealing message. [S11, S12]",
"Keep first response distinct from ongoing case management. A respectful conversation and an agreed referral may be a useful contribution, while continued assessment, safety planning and case records require qualified roles, supervision and organisational systems. Ask whether the intended support was actually reachable only through an agreed appropriate follow-up process. A sent referral is an output, not proof that safety, recovery or wellbeing improved. Provider-perspective research supports attention to continuity and system barriers but does not show that more contacts are always beneficial or that a worker should pursue someone who has declined further contact. [R22, S11, S1]",
"When a pathway fails, discuss an appropriate next option with the person through the agreed process rather than blame them or hide the gap. A general service-quality concern can be raised using necessary non-identifying information and appropriate consultation, with permission and safeguards where needed. Preserve a route to ask questions or complain without making disclosure a condition of assistance. In this course, use only the fictional handover exercise and assess the clarity of consent, scope, current service information and contact arrangements. Independent supervised skills observation is still needed before a real professional role can be considered competent. [S12, S13, S1]"],
"Fictional case: an adult accepts a referral but does not want messages on a shared phone. The first responder checks the permitted handover route, shares only necessary agreed information and does not create a personal case file or promise ongoing case management. The receiving service's response remains to be confirmed.",
"Draft a fictional handover with purpose, receiving role, agreed information, communication boundary, unconfirmed condition and an authorised feedback route. Explain what a sent referral cannot demonstrate.",
"A good handover protects the agreed boundaries and identifies what remains unresolved. It avoids revealing contact, parallel case records and claims that a referral alone proves recovery or specialist competence.",["S11","S12","S1","R22","S13"],[
("Handover information should match the agreed purpose.","A receiving contact needs a limited access enquiry. What information approach is appropriate?","Use the necessary agreed information through the authorised pathway.",["Send the whole narrative to every partner by default.","Publish identifiers to prove a referral occurred.","Keep a duplicate personal incident database."],"Referral does not remove purpose and information limits."),
("Communication safety depends on the person and context.","An adult says a phone is shared and messages are unsafe. What should the worker do?","Respect that boundary and use only a confirmed appropriate contact arrangement.",["Leave a revealing voicemail because follow-up is important.","Assume a generic message can never expose the service relationship.","Use an unrelated administrative number without checking."],"Even the fact of service contact can be sensitive."),
("First response differs from ongoing case management.","A learner offers basic support and referral. What is needed before undertaking specialist ongoing casework?","The appropriate qualified role, supervision, organisational systems and competency development.",["Only a completed referral form.","A promise to keep all records on a personal phone.","An assumption that one MCQ pass establishes professional competence."],"Specialist practice requires more than an introductory response lesson."),
("A referral sent is not a demonstrated wellbeing outcome.","A team reports recovery because a referral message was delivered. What is a defensible statement?","The referral was sent; access and outcomes require separate appropriate evidence.",["Delivery proves recovery.","No receiving response matters.","A message count certifies safety."],"An output should not be presented as a verified personal outcome."),
("A failed pathway requires an accountable response.","The agreed receiving service cannot provide the requested support. What should happen next?","Use the agreed appropriate process to explain the gap and consider feasible options with the person.",["Blame the person for the service limitation.","Hide the failed pathway in a success report.","Make an unverified admission promise elsewhere."],"A useful response is honest, bounded and informed by the person's preferences."),
]),
])
